0089874267578125, -0.00753021240234375, 0.0099029541015625, -0.0198974609375, 0.0268402099609375, -0.022186279296875, -0.004924774169921875, -0.033599853515625, -0.0283203125, -0.014190673828125, -0.0268096923828125, 0.042236328125, 0.021392822265625, 0.0206298828125, 0.015899658203125, 0.0150146484375, 0.00945281982421875, 0.010223388671875, -0.05316162109375, -0.0018310546875, -0.007022857666015625, -0.0036830902099609375, 0.039642333984375, -0.0282745361328125, 0.004978179931640625, -0.021942138671875, -0.009674072265625, 0.0001220703125, 0.006877899169921875, 0.011749267578125, 0.016387939453125, 0.0010881423950195312, 0.01137542724609375, -0.0061492919921875, 0.0294647216796875, -0.00861358642578125, 0.02313232421875, -0.007503509521484375, 0.0090789794921875, 0.01064300537109375, -0.0227508544921875, 3.081560134887695e-05, -0.0099334716796875, -0.0179595947265625, -0.0172271728515625, -0.01302337646484375, 0.0254364013671875, -0.035125732421875, 0.011016845703125, 0.004116058349609375, 0.0157470703125, -0.006954193115234375, -0.0067901611328125, -0.015350341796875, 0.01187896728515625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nHow level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In level-k models, \"level-0 actions\" refer to choices made by players who do not engage in strategic reasoning; these actions are typically assumed to be non-strategic, such as random selection or choosing salient or focal options. \"Level-0 beliefs\" are the expectations that these players hold about the actions of others, which are also presumed to be non-strategic and uninformed by higher-order reasoning. Understanding the formation and alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs is crucial for accurately modeling behavior in strategic settings.\n\nThis study addresses two main research questions: (1) How are level-0 actions and beliefs formed, and do they align within individuals? (2) How do these patterns differ across games, and what are the implications for the assumptions underlying standard level-k models? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that standard level-k assumptions may not hold uniformly across different strategic settings.\n\nWe selected the canonical beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game for our experiments because they represent distinct strategic environments commonly used in the literature to study bounded rationality. The beauty contest game is widely regarded as a benchmark for eliciting non-strategic (level-0) behavior due to its simple structure and clear focal point, while the 11-20 game introduces more complex incentives and potential for strategic reasoning, allowing us to test the robustness of level-0 assumptions across different contexts.\n\nMethods\n\nTo investigate these questions, we employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014). In this approach, participants are grouped into teams and required to reach a consensus on their choices through open discussion. These discussions are audio-recorded and transcribed for analysis. We coded the transcripts to identify instances of level-0 reasoning, operationalized as explicit statements or arguments indicating non-strategic choice (e.g., \"just pick the middle number,\" \"choose randomly,\" or \"pick the lowest number because it stands out\"), as well as corresponding beliefs about others' choices that reflect a lack of strategic adjustment. Two independent coders reviewed all transcripts, and discrepancies were resolved through discussion. Inter-rater reliability, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, was 0.87, indicating high agreement.\n\nOur study involves two experimental games, each conducted with independent samples of undergraduate students recruited from a university subject pool. In total, 120 participants (60 per game) took part in the experiments. Participants were randomly assigned to teams of three, resulting in 20 teams per game. Each team played the assigned game after receiving standardized instructions. Demographic information was collected, with the majority of participants aged 18\u201325 and balanced in gender representation.\n\nGame Selection and Design\n\nFirst, in the canonical beauty contest game, players are asked to choose a number between 0 and 100, aiming to guess two-thirds of the average number chosen by all participants. This game is frequently used to study iterative reasoning and serves as a baseline for non-strategic (level-0) behavior, as the focal point of 50 is salient in the absence of strategic thinking. Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) previously found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard level-k assumption that level-0 players act non-strategically and are perceived as such by others.\n\nSecond, we study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). In the standard version, each player selects an integer between 11 and 20, and a player receives a payoff only if their chosen number is exactly one less than another player's choice. In our variant, we modified the payoff structure so that the reward for choosing a number exactly one less than another\u2019s choice was doubled, and a small bonus was added for choosing the lowest unique number. This design increases the incentive for strategic reasoning while retaining the core mechanics, allowing us to examine whether level-0 reasoning persists or changes under more complex incentives.\n\nResults\n\nIn the beauty contest game, 18% of teams (i.e., 4 out of 20 teams) exhibited level-0 reasoning based on transcript coding. Within these teams, the mean chosen number was 50 (SD = 2.1), and their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices also centered around 50 (mean = 49.8, SD = 2.4). A paired-sample t-test revealed no significant difference between level-0 actions and beliefs within these teams (t(3) = 0.23, p = 0.83), confirming the correspondence between beliefs and actions for level-0 players in this context.\n\nIn the 11-20 game variant, 15% of teams (3 out of 20 teams) were classified as exhibiting level-0 reasoning based on their discussions. For these teams, the mean chosen number was 15.2 (SD = 2.8), while their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices averaged 17.6 (SD = 1.9). A paired-sample t-test indicated a significant difference between actions and beliefs (t(2) = -4.24, p = 0.05), and both means differed from the standard level-0 assumption of choosing the lowest available number (11). This quantitative divergence suggests that the standard level-0 specification may not fully capture the reasoning processes in this setting and points to the need for alternative or additional explanations beyond those previously proposed in the literature.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe observed divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game variant is significant for level-k modeling because it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is both non-strategic and commonly understood by higher-level players. Our findings suggest that level-0 reasoning may be influenced by contextual cues and payoff structures, and that higher-level players may misperceive or mispredict level-0 behavior in certain games. To refine level-k models, researchers might consider incorporating context-dependent definitions of level-0 behavior, such as allowing the focal point or salient features of the game to determine level-0 choices, or modeling heterogeneity in non-strategic reasoning across games. Among alternative explanations, the influence of focal points (e.g., salient numbers like 15 in the 11-20 game) and heuristic-based reasoning (e.g., \"choose a number in the middle\" or \"avoid extremes\") appear particularly promising for capturing observed discrepancies.\n\nLimitations\n\nLimitations of our study include the use of a student sample and a limited set of games, which may affect generalizability. Additionally, the team-based discussion format may introduce biases, as group dynamics could influence the expression or suppression of individual reasoning styles, potentially leading to consensus choices that do not fully reflect individual level-0 reasoning. The process of reaching consensus may also encourage more deliberative or strategic thinking than would occur in individual decision-making. Future research could examine a broader range of games, participant populations, and alternative elicitation methods (such as individual think-aloud protocols) to further clarify the formation and perception of level-0 reasoning.\n\nConclusion\n\nIn summary, our study demonstrates that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions is context-dependent, supporting standard assumptions in some games but not others, and underscores the need for more nuanced modeling of boundedly rational behavior in strategic settings. By providing a detailed account of how level-0 reasoning is elicited and classified, and by highlighting the influence of game context and group dynamics, we contribute to a more precise understanding of the foundations and limitations of level-k models."}}